  $str$584$str$,
  $str$Joint Exhibition: Calligraphy in CHINA-JAPAN-KOREA$str$,
  $str$국립한글박물관$str$,
  $str$Special Exhibition Hall$str$,
  null,
  $str$<h6>Introduction</h6>  Opening the Special Exhibition on the Calligraphies of Korea, China, and JapanThe history of writing traces back to pictograms around 3000 BC. Nowadays, after going through numerous times of changes in their shapes and features, a lot of writing systems are currently used across the globe. The Culture of three countries – China, Japan, and Korea – have been mixed in harmony on the basis of the Chinese characters, the writing system that all three countries share, and the writing brush, the 2,000-year-old medium of record. This special exhibition intends to compare the beauty pursued in each writing system by looking into the three countries’ representative calligraphy works. Through this experience, the audience will be able to feel the balance between the left and right, the top and bottom, the way of structuring the shape of a character, and the strength and energy from each stroke. This exhibition will serve as an opportunity to experience one’s original self. <h6>Composition</h6>    <h5 class="subTabCmu1">Part 1. China</h5> <!-- subTabCContent --> Part 1. China – Chinese Script of Long and Rich History Setting aside Cangjie, a legendary figure in ancient China who was a historian of the Yellow Emperor and the inventor of the Chinese characters, or symbols carved on potteries with bones or pieces or iron, the history of Chinese characters starts from around 1500 BC. Its origins were inscriptions on oracle bones or bronze. Throughout the Cao Wei dynasty, the Jin dynasty, and the northern and southern dynasties various types of semi-cursive scripts appeared through Zhong Yao, Lu Ji, and Wang Xizhi. Later, during the Sui, the Tang, and the Eastern Wu dynasty (589 – 960), talented artists such as Ouyang Xun, Yu Shi Nan, Chu Sui Liang, Zhang Xu, Yan Zhen Ging, and Hai Su completed the square-style, semi-cursive, and cursive script, helping the era of fancy script flourish. The long and rich history of Chinese scripts will display the way different strokes make one style distinguishable from another.   ▲ Inscription on tortoise plastron and animal bone  ▲ Song celebrating the opening of a road through the rugged Xixia County   ▲ Lanting Xu (Preface of the Orchid Pavilion) by Wang Xizhi <!-- // subTabCContent -->  <h5 class="subTabCmu2">Part 2. Japan</h5> <!-- subTabCContent -->Scripts of Kana, Practical Writing System Japanese calligraphy started when Ajiki and Wang In from Baekje spread Chinese characters to Japan. Japan established its unique culture based on the calligraphies of China and Korea. The most distinctive feature of Japanese calligraphy is Kana calligraphy. It takes its foundations from Man'yōgana, a writing system that employs Chinese characters to represent the Japanese language. Kana went through a simplification process, which is well portrayed in 『Akihagijyo』. After the transitional period going from Man'yōgana, to Sōgana, then finally to Hiragana, Kana calligraphy became a popular art in Japan. The Japanese calligraphy of today includes dynamic variations ranging from Kana, to Kanji (Chinese characters), to avant-garde calligraphy, to daijisho. Now the scope of calligraphy has broadened even to calligraphy designs in our daily life and digital calligraphy.  ▲ Oldest existing collection of Japanese poetry  ▲ Genji Monogatari (The Tale of Genji) <!-- // subTabCContent -->  <h5 class="subTabCmu3">Part 3. Korea</h5> <!-- subTabCContent -->Variation of Hangeul Script Style, After the Invention of Hangeul It is well known that the history of Hangeul scripts starts from the invention of Hangeul. Go-che looks similar to Panbon-che that was scribed on plates. Its strokes are drawn in consistent thickness, and the shape of a character is rectangular with perfect balance between the left and the right. Kung-che originated from the royal palace during the Joseon dynasty. It is categorized into two styles: the square style and the cursive style. The square style is neat and sophisticated, while the cursive style boasts its beauty depending on the strength and speed of the strokes. Min-che is a style used among the general public since Hangeul’s publication. It is free of formality, somewhat rough but rich in emotion and rhythm. These various Hangeul scripts have taken root as the ones of the present time after going through tumultuous changes of printing culture in the modern times, such as leading, phototypesetting, and computer typesetting.   ▲ Collection of lyrics wishing for Joseon dynasty’s prosperity  ▲ Book categorizing the standards of good and evil behavior   ▲ Handwritten fiction novel sent home by Empress Hyoui  ▲ Hangeul novel, Okrangjajeon <!-- // subTabCContent -->  <h6>Photo</h6> <!-- tab -->    Photo 1    Photo 2    Photo 3    Photo 4    Photo 5    Photo 6           <!-- #program_preview{display:inline-block;margin:30px 0;font-family:NanumGothic,나눔고딕,나눔 고딕,맑은 고딕,dotum;font-size:12px;} #program_preview > .poster_img{float:left;display:inline-block;margin-right:20px;width:298px; border:1px inset #ccc} #program_preview > .description {float:right;width:367px;} #program_preview > .description li {border-bottom:1px solid #ccc} .stitle{font-weight:bold;font-size: 1.25em; color:#355b78;background-color: #fff;} .stitle > p{display:inline-block;padding:4px 5px;margin-right:5px;font-weight:bold;font-size:1em;color:#fff;background-color:#4dafd3;} #program_preview > .description li > h6{font-weight:bold;font-size:1.8em;line-height:24px;margin:11px 0;color:#2b2b2b;} #program_preview > .description li > dl{padding:9px 0;font-size:1.2em} #program_preview > .description li > dl dt{float:left;display:inline-block;width:100px;font-weight:bold;} #program_preview > .description li > dl > dd > dl {display:inline-block;width:auto;margin:-10px 0 0 1px;border-bottom:1px solid #ccc;font-size:0.9em} #program_preview > .description li > dl > dd > dl > dt, #program_preview > .description li > dl > dd > dl > dd{padding:14px 2px 9px 0;} #program_preview > .description li > dl > dd > dl > dt{width:45px;display:inline-block;font-weight:normal;margin:0;} #program_preview > .description li > dl > dd > dl > dd{width:205px;margin-left:46px;font-weight:bold;} .exhibition_composition {display:block;margin:10px 0 30px 0; border:1px solid #ccc;padding:12px;font-size:1.2em;line-height:1.7em;font-family:NanumGothic,나눔고딕,나눔 고딕,맑은 고딕,dotum;} h6{display:block;overflow:hidden;margin:40px 0 0px 1px;font-size:1.3em;border-top:0;font-family:NanumGothic,나눔고딕,나눔 고딕,맑은 고딕,dotum;} .exhibition_composition ul{margin:10px 0 0 0;padding:0} .exhibition_composition ul li{border-bottom:1px dashed #ccc;list-style-type:none;list-style-position:inside} .exhibition_composition p{margin:20px auto;padding:0 9px;} .exhibition_composition .linktype1{color:#00f} .exhibition_composition .linktype1:hover{text-decoration:underline} .exhibition_composition .text_small{color:#000;} #subTabC{position:relative;display:inline-block;margin:10px 0 0px 0;border:1px solid #ccc;font-family:NanumGothic,나눔고딕,나눔 고딕,맑은 고딕,dotum;} #subTabC ul{} #subTabC ul li{width:99%;float:left;list-style:none} #subTabC ul li h5{position:absolute;top:20px;padding-left:25px;font-size:15px;color:#555;font-weight:bold;height:40px;width:30%;text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid #dddddd;cursor:pointer;word-break: keep-all} #subTabC ul li.on h5{color:#3D578A;border-bottom:2px solid #3D578A} #subTabC .subTabCContent{padding:110px 30px 30px;font-size:1.20em;line-height:1.7em;} #subTabC .stitle{margin-top:30px;padding:30px 0 10px 0;font-weight:bold;font-size:1.25em;color:#3D578A;border-top:1px dashed #ccc} #subTabC .subTabCContent .image_area{margin:0;font-size:12px;line-height:1.45em;} #subTabC .subTabCContent .image_area img{display:block;margin:4px 0 0 0; width:99.9%} #subTabC .subTabCContent .image_area span, #subTabC .subTabCContent .image_area strong{display:block;font-size:13px;margin:10px 0 4px 0;font-family:NanumGothic,나눔고딕,나눔 고딕,맑은 고딕,dotum;} #subTabC ul li h5.subTabCmu1{left:0;} #subTabC ul li h5.subTabCmu2{left:33%;} #subTabC ul li h5.subTabCmu3{left:66%;} .box_gray1{width:;margin:10px 0;padding:19px 14px;background-color:#f4f4f4;font-size:0.9em} .stitle2{color:#06438e} #photo{position:relative;display:block;margin:10px 0 0px 0;height:400px;width:99.9%;} #photo .photoContent{display:block;text-align:center;vertical-align:top} #photo .photoContent img{display:block; width:99.9%} #photo ul{text-align:center} #photo ul li{float:left;list-style:none;background:none;text-align:right} #photo ul li a {position:absolute;display:block;top:10px;margin:0;height:20px;width:16px; text-indent:-100px; overflow:hidden;cursor:pointer;background:url(http://www.hangeul.go.kr/images/new/slide_dot.png) 0px 2px no-repeat;} #photo ul li.onn a{background:url(http://www.hangeul.go.kr/images/new/slide_dot.png) -16px 2px no-repeat;} #photo ul li .photomu1{left:0;} #photo ul li .photomu2{left:20px} #photo ul li .photomu3{left:40px} #photo ul li .photomu4{left:60px} #photo ul li .photomu5{left:80px} #photo ul li .photomu6{left:100px} #photo ul li .photomu7{left:120px} #photo ul li .photomu8{left:140px;} #photo ul li .photomu9{left:160px} #photo ul li .photomu10{left:180px} #photo ul li .photomu11{left:200px} #photo ul li .photomu12{left:220px} #photo ul li .photomu13{left:240px} #photo ul li .photomu14{left:260px} #photo ul li .photomu15{left:280px} -->   $(document).ready(function () { $(".subTabCContent").hide(); $(".subTabCContent:first").show(); $("#subTabC li h5").bind("click", function (e) { e.preventDefault(); $("#subTabC li").removeClass("on"); $("#subTabC li .subTabCContent").css("display", "none"); $(this).parent().addClass("on"); $(this).next().css("display", "block"); }); }); $(document).ready(function () { $(".photoContent").hide(); $(".photoContent:first").show(); $("#photo li a").bind("click", function (e) { e.preventDefault(); $("#photo li").removeClass("onn"); $("#photo li .photoContent").css("display", "none"); $(this).parent().addClass("onn"); $(this).next().css("display", "block"); }); });$str$,
