  $str$486$str$,
  $str$Write, revise, and rewrite : Hangeul in Novels$str$,
  $str$국립한글박물관$str$,
  $str$Special Exhibition Hall$str$,
  null,
  $str$<h6>Introduction</h6>   In general, writers follow a process of writing, revising and rewriting over and over, as they work, in order to find words turn into a fine piece of writing. It is the power of words that makes readers sometimes cry or smile and engages their curiosity and allows them to enjoy reading often from dawn to dusk.  This exhibition presents the beauty of the Korean alphabet, Hangeul, and displays the importance of writing with a large number of novels written in Korean. Moreover, the exhibition aims at enabling visitors to experience the power of Hangeul, and hopefully to share some of the feeling and emotions native Korean speakers find in novels through the hard work of their authors.  The exhibit hall consists of three parts, following the process of the work of a writer. The first section is the writer’s thinking room, where he or she gets ideas, outlines a novel and starts to write. The second section is the revising room, where authors review, evaluate and make changes to their writing. The last section is the polishing room, where the author looks for exactly the right words and retouches sentences to put them at their best. In addition, this exhibition introduces novelists who try to maintain and use our unique language in correct way, making it beautiful at the same time. Also, the exhibition shows timeless novels loved by Koreans, generation after generation. The exhibition also shows how various cultural phenomena are derived from much-loved Korean novels. In the exhibition hall, there are spaces where visitors can read various novels freely and have hands-on experience with the Hangeul alphabet. Through this exhibition, we hope that our visitors will come to understand how greatly Hangeul has enriched Korean culture across the centuries and how this extraordinary language and writing system has in turn enriched the works of our great writers.  <h6>Photo</h6><!-- tab -->    <h5 class="subTabCmu1"></h5>    <h5 class="subTabCmu2"><